---
/**
 * 2026-ballot.astro — the 2026 council-structure ballot explainer.
 * Ported from design-concepts/ballot.html.
 *
 * ⚠ Phase A: copy is DRAFT and must be verified against the certified ballot
 * language before launch (see the brief). It is a live political topic — kept
 * nonpartisan and dated. The draft banner below is removed once copy is certified.
 */
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const b = base.endsWith("/") ? base : base + "/";
---
<Layout
  title="How Columbus elects its City Council is on the 2026 ballot"
  description="A nonpartisan explainer of the 2026 Columbus ballot measure on how City Council is elected — the current hybrid system vs. true single-member districts."
  ribbon={false}
>
  <div class="draftbar">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 9v4M12 17h.01M10.3 3.9 1.8 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.7 3h17a2 2 0 0 0 1.7-3L13.7 3.9a2 2 0 0 0-3.4 0Z"/></svg>
    <span><strong>Draft &mdash; pending certified ballot language.</strong> This copy is being verified against the final certified measure and may change before launch.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap"><div class="article">
    <section class="art-hero">
      <div class="eyebrow">Ballot Measure &middot; General Election, November 4, 2026</div>
      <h1>How Columbus elects its City Council is on the ballot</h1>
      <p class="dek">This November, Columbus voters decide whether to keep the current hybrid system &mdash; where every voter helps elect all nine council members &mdash; or switch to true single-member districts, where you vote only for the member who represents your own district.</p>
      <div class="asof"><span class="dot"></span> As of August 2026 &middot; details may change before the final ballot is certified</div>
      <div class="keyfacts">
        <div class="kf"><div class="kfl">What it is</div><div class="kfv">A proposed city charter amendment</div></div>
        <div class="kf"><div class="kfl">How it qualified</div><div class="kfv">Citizen petition &mdash; 23,000+ signatures</div></div>
        <div class="kf"><div class="kfl">When you vote</div><div class="kfv">Tuesday, November 4, 2026</div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="callout"><b>This is a developing story.</b> City Council and the petition organizers have been discussing a possible compromise. What ultimately appears on the ballot &mdash; and its exact wording &mdash; may change. This page will be updated as the ballot is finalized. Every claim here links to a primary source.</div>

    <h2>The two systems, side by side</h2>
    <p>Both systems use nine geographic districts, and in both, a council member must live in the district they represent. The difference is <strong>who gets to vote for each seat</strong>.</p>
    <div class="compare2">
      <div class="sys today">
        <div class="sh"><div class="lab">Today &middot; in place since 2023</div><div class="nm">Hybrid at-large districts</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Who votes for your member</div><div class="sa">Every voter in Columbus votes in all nine district races.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Races on your ballot</div><div class="sa">All nine council seats, every cycle they're up.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Who members answer to</div><div class="sa">The whole city &mdash; every member is elected citywide.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">The argument for it</div><div class="sa">Members stay accountable to all of Columbus, not just one district.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sys prop">
        <div class="sh"><div class="lab">Proposed &middot; the ballot measure</div><div class="nm">True single-member districts</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Who votes for your member</div><div class="sa">Only residents of your district vote for your representative.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Races on your ballot</div><div class="sa">One council race &mdash; the seat for your district.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Who members answer to</div><div class="sa">Their own district's residents directly.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">The argument for it</div><div class="sa">Neighborhoods get a representative accountable to them specifically.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>How we got here</h2>
    <div class="timeline">
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">2018</div><div class="ty">Voters approve districts</div><div class="td">Columbus ends its century-old fully at-large council, adopting the hybrid district system and expanding council from seven seats to nine.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">2023</div><div class="ty">First district elections</div><div class="td">All nine district seats are filled for the first time under the new system.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">2025</div><div class="ty">Second cycle</div><div class="td">Districts 1, 3, 4, and 7 are up; the staggered four-year terms take hold.</div></div>
      <div class="tl now"><div class="tdate">2026</div><div class="ty">A petition qualifies for the ballot</div><div class="td">The group Our City Our Say gathers more than 23,000 signatures &mdash; clearing the validation threshold &mdash; to put single-member districts before voters. Council weighs a possible compromise.</div></div>
      <div class="tl future"><div class="tdate">November 4, 2026</div><div class="ty">Columbus votes</div><div class="td">The measure goes before city voters.</div></div>
      <div class="tl future"><div class="tdate">If it passes</div><div class="ty">A new map and new rules</div><div class="td">The city would implement the change on the timeline set by the amendment.</div></div>
    </div>

    <h2>The compromise being discussed</h2>
    <p>Rather than a straight yes-or-no on single-member districts, City Council and some organizers have floated a middle path: <strong>expand the council</strong> with several new at-large seats alongside nine ward seats, pair it with <strong>campaign-finance reform</strong>, and <strong>redraw the district map</strong>. Supporters of a deal argue it delivers neighborhood representation without losing citywide accountability; some petition backers counter that it dilutes the original proposal. Whether a compromise reaches the ballot &mdash; and in what form &mdash; was still unsettled as of late August 2026.</p>

    <h2>What each side says</h2>
    <div class="procon">
      <div class="pc for">
        <h3>Supporters of single-member districts</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Neighborhoods get a representative who answers to them directly, not the whole city.</li>
          <li>Running for a single district costs less than a citywide campaign, lowering the barrier to entry.</li>
          <li>It's how most large U.S. cities elect their councils.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="pc against">
        <h3>Those who favor the current system</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Every member being elected citywide keeps the whole council accountable to all of Columbus.</li>
          <li>Voters keep a say in all nine seats rather than just one.</li>
          <li>The hybrid system is only two cycles old &mdash; too soon to judge, some argue.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:.86rem;color:var(--ink-3);">The points above summarize positions publicly stated by each side; they are not endorsements. Columbus Districts is a nonpartisan reference and does not take a position on the measure.</p>

    <h2>Make your plan to vote</h2>
    <div class="vote-cta">
      <div class="vc"><div class="vk">Step 1</div><div class="vv">Check your registration</div><a href="https://www.ohiosos.gov/elections/voters/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ohio Secretary of State &rarr;</a></div>
      <div class="vc"><div class="vk">Step 2</div><div class="vv">Find your district</div><a href={`${b}all-districts/`}>Look up your address &rarr;</a></div>
      <div class="vc"><div class="vk">Step 3</div><div class="vv">Know the key dates</div><a href="https://boe.franklincountyohio.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Franklin County BOE &rarr;</a></div>
    </div>
  </div></div>
</Layout>
